{"kind":"report","text":"Approved exact head 84cf40acd458917300393d6bd5fa678d6dc52cca, complete republished artifact set, after checking the streaming repair and its integration with the landed receipt lane.\n\nThe header-progress defect in review git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:42e9191f958f704ed753161fbf5b6c289805e3d0 is fixed: StreamJSON renews its existing idle timer after receiving headers, giving the first body byte a full idle interval. The committed TestStreamJSONCountsHeadersAsProgress and my independent earlier reproducer both pass; removing that one renewal makes both named tests fail, with race checking. The earlier fixed-size and depth-scaled ceilings remain removed.\n\nArchitecture: layer 7 command/transport composition changes from a bounded full-response/whole-read budget to an identity-first, idle-bounded streaming read. The same head includes the architecture artifact describing the changed contract, frontier-only trust and unbounded decoded-memory cost. Layers 2, 4 and 5 retain local verification, signing admission and fold authority. The receipt-lane merge preserves both command paths; no additional protocol or profile change occurs.\n\nSecurity: the bounded summary checks genesis, local head, unmoved frontier and matching cursor before any unbounded response read. The complete response is checked against the head captured before the entire operation; session reads retain fold-profile and decision-count checks. Streaming shares the loopback and redirect policy, rejects unknown fields and trailing values, limits refusal bodies and cancels silence before headers or during the body. Headers now count as progress. The summary is a consistency check, not cryptographic authentication of rows; the architecture correctly says those rows remain the trusted resident's assertion. Memory and a peer that continuously sends data have no total cap, which is stated inside the existing trusted-process posture. Local signing admission still supplies durable authority. No unresolved defect found.\n\nSimplification: a shared fetch and the existing idle timer replace the ineffective average-record-size formula. The three-line header renewal fixes the missing transition without adding another timer or a whole-read ceiling. No unnecessary mechanism to remove.\n\nIndependent checks at this exact source: the full residentclient package, including the committed new regression and my original header probe, passed with race checking (5.546 s). Focused large step-command, identity-before-read and moved-frontier integration tests passed (6.845 s). The earlier 3ceb9466 source had already passed the original legal 150-by-512-KiB history (depth151, 78,751,005-byte snapshot, 23.212 s), the committed 80-by-900-KiB regression, the wider command gates (48.407 s), client race tests and vet. The code difference from that source is the header renewal/regression, removal of the unsupported RSS figure and the previously reviewed receipt-lane integration; those earlier results are carried evidence, not claimed as fresh runs of the repaired head.\n\nTen compiling omission controls were checked across the repair: ignore identity refusal; omit final frontier recheck; restore the byte ceiling; stop renewing the body idle timer; stop cancelling on silence; permit unknown fields; permit trailing JSON; remove the refusal-body bound; shorten identity time to the display budget; and omit header renewal. Each made its intended named test red. The refusal-body guard was tested by an independent test that first passed. The header omission was tested against both the committed and independent regressions. All controls ran in disposable exact-source archives and were restored.\n\nRead the retained exact-head full-suite log gotest-84cf40a.log; its result is attributed to that run, not to an independent rerun of every package. No new production-sized timing or RSS campaign was run by this reviewer. The unsupported historical 1.87 GB figure is removed from this head; the real artroom measurement and decoder-shape example remain stated without a universal bound. This approval adopts no memory/latency guarantee and authorizes no resident rollout. It answers the size-cliff request, including its explicit reference-resolution scope clarification; the signing-fold task remains separate.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:21e475f5387ba5b851a646eb64278023a5a73430","binding":"assigned","head":"84cf40acd458917300393d6bd5fa678d6dc52cca","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:c1c96ebf4cf7c5c6410cae53570db5795adaba52\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:cddc4fccd937c3532b3aa4e39784577e0e8d5213\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:c1c96ebf4cf7c5c6410cae53570db5795adaba52","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}